WEBVTT
TITLE Local Optimisation and Basic Blocks: Leaders, DAGs and the Six Safe Rewrites — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Local optimisation looks at one basic block at a time.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Inside a block there is no branch, so one scan knows every value.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Fold, propagate, reuse, simplify, cheapen, delete — all decidable here.

4
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.000
A leader is the first instruction, a jump target, or what follows a jump.

5
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Nine instructions, five leaders, five blocks.

6
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:00.000
A block is maximal: no jump lands inside it and it exits only at the end.

7
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Seventeen instructions, three jumps, and a nested loop.

8
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Six leaders: 1001, 1002, 1003, 1010, 1012, 1013.

9
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Eight edges between blocks, and two blocks that jump to themselves.

10
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Fifteen instructions, three jumps, one nested loop.

11
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Seven blocks, and their sizes are 2, 1, 6, 1, 2, 2, 1.

12
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Seven blocks, largest six instructions — option D.

13
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Five statements, but only three distinct computations.

14
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Six nodes: three leaves and three operators; six edges.

15
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Read the convention: with leaves six nodes, without them three.

16
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Folding, constant propagation and copy propagation move values, not operators.

17
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Common subexpression, algebraic identity, strength reduction change the operator.

18
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Nine statements become four, and the order of the passes matters.

19
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Scan backwards from the live-out set, and strike what nobody reads.

20
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:20.000
z is dead, t2 is dead, the copies are dead; x and y are live out.

21
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:30.000
A store through a pointer is never dead, however unused it looks.

22
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Reassociation is false for floating point, and true for integers.

23
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:50.000
x / 2 is not x >> 1 when x is negative.

24
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:00.000
x * 0 is not 0 when x is NaN, and 1 / 0 must not be folded.

25
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:10.000
B1 computes a + b; B2 computes it again and cannot tell.

26
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Available expressions over the graph does see it, on one path.

27
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:30.000
The 2021 count: three hundred and three additions, two dereferences.

28
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Five steps: leaders, cut, edges, rewrite, re-count.

29
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Three traps: backward targets, the line after a jump, and the exit node.

30
00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Local is the half a block can justify; the rest is data-flow analysis.

